      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# LLM Training: How a Model Learns — Step-by-Step\n",
        "\n",
        "We will learn **LLM Training from Step 1 to Step 12**, in order. I will keep each concept separate and use one example throughout.\n",
        "\n",
        "Our example sentence:\n",
        "\n",
        "> **\"The cat sat on the mat.\"**\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine we are training an LLM to predict the next word in a sentence.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 1: Training Data\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is training data?\n",
        "\n",
        "Training data is the collection of examples from which an LLM learns patterns in language.\n",
        "\n",
        "It can contain:\n",
        "- Books\n",
        "- Websites\n",
        "- Articles\n",
        "- Conversations\n",
        "- Programming code\n",
        "- Educational content\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose our training dataset contains:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "The cat sat on the mat.\n",
        "The dog played in the garden.\n",
        "The sun rises in the east.\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The model processes many examples like these to learn patterns in language.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What does the model learn?\n",
        "\n",
        "It learns statistical patterns such as:\n",
        "- Which words commonly appear together.\n",
        "- How sentences are structured.\n",
        "- How words relate to their context.\n",
        "- How to predict likely continuations.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:** Training data is not automatically stored as a database of facts inside the model. Training adjusts the model's parameters to learn patterns from the data.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:** Training data provides the examples from which the model learns.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 2: Tokenization\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is tokenization?\n",
        "\n",
        "Tokenization converts text into smaller units called **tokens**.\n",
        "\n",
        "A token might be:\n",
        "- A complete word\n",
        "- Part of a word\n",
        "- Punctuation\n",
        "- Another text unit, depending on the tokenizer\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's use this sentence:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "The cat sat on the mat.\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For simplicity, imagine our tokenizer splits it like this:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Input:\n",
        "\"The cat sat on the mat.\"\n",
        "\n",
        "          ↓\n",
        "\n",
        "Tokens:\n",
        "[\"The\", \"cat\", \"sat\", \"on\", \"the\", \"mat\", \".\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "          ↓\n",
        "\n",
        "Token IDs:\n",
        "[101, 205, 314, 42, 101, 892, 13]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "**These token IDs are illustrative, not actual IDs from a specific model.**\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is a token ID?\n",
        "\n",
        "A token ID is a numerical identifier assigned to a token by the tokenizer's vocabulary.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "\"The\" → 101\n",
        "\"cat\" → 205\n",
        "\"sat\" → 314\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The numbers are identifiers. The number `205` does not mean that \"cat\" has a value of 205.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why do we need tokenization?\n",
        "\n",
        "Neural networks process numerical representations rather than raw text characters directly.\n",
        "\n",
        "Tokenization provides the numerical IDs that the model can use.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:**\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Text → Tokens → Token IDs\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 3: Embedding Lookup\n",
        "\n",
        "Now we have token IDs.\n",
        "\n",
        "What happens next?\n",
        "\n",
        "The model converts each token ID into a vector using an **embedding table**.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 3.1 What is an embedding?\n",
        "\n",
        "An embedding is a list of numerical values representing a token.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, imagine the embedding table contains:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Token ID: 205\n",
        "Token:    \"cat\"\n",
        "\n",
        "Embedding vector:\n",
        "[0.6, 0.2, -0.4, 0.8]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a simplified example with only four numbers. Real models typically use vectors with hundreds or thousands of dimensions.\n",
        "\n",
        "Another token might have a different vector:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Token: \"dog\"\n",
        "\n",
        "Embedding vector:\n",
        "[0.5, 0.3, -0.2, 0.7]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "These values are learned during training.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 3.2 What is an embedding table?\n",
        "\n",
        "Think of it as a large table:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Token ID | Token | Example embedding |\n",
        "|---:|---|---|\n",
        "| 101 | The | `[0.2, 0.1, 0.4, ...]` |\n",
        "| 205 | cat | `[0.6, 0.2, -0.4, ...]` |\n",
        "| 314 | sat | `[0.3, -0.1, 0.7, ...]` |\n",
        "| 42 | on | `[0.1, 0.5, 0.2, ...]` |\n",
        "\n",
        "When the model receives token ID `205`, the embedding layer retrieves the corresponding vector.\n",
        "\n",
        "This operation is called **embedding lookup**.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 3.3 How are embedding values learned?\n",
        "\n",
        "Initially, embedding values are commonly initialized using a suitable initialization scheme.\n",
        "\n",
        "During training:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. The model makes predictions.\n",
        "2. The loss measures its prediction errors.\n",
        "3. Backpropagation calculates gradients.\n",
        "4. The optimizer updates the model's parameters, including the embedding parameters.\n",
        "\n",
        "Over many training steps, the embeddings become useful numerical representations.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:**\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Token ID → Embedding Lookup → Vector\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 4: Positional Information\n",
        "\n",
        "We now have embedding vectors for our tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "But there is another problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "**How does the model represent the order of tokens?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider these sentences:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Sentence A:\n",
        "The cat chased the dog.\n",
        "\n",
        "Sentence B:\n",
        "The dog chased the cat.\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The words are similar, but the meanings differ because their positions differ.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4.1 Why is positional information needed?\n",
        "\n",
        "Self-attention processes relationships between tokens. By itself, standard self-attention does not inherently encode the sequence order.\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore, Transformer models need a mechanism to represent positional information.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4.2 How does it work?\n",
        "\n",
        "Conceptually:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Token embedding\n",
        "       +\n",
        "Positional information\n",
        "       |\n",
        "       ▼\n",
        "Position-aware representation\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Token: \"The\"\n",
        "Position: 1\n",
        "\n",
        "Token: \"cat\"\n",
        "Position: 2\n",
        "\n",
        "Token: \"sat\"\n",
        "Position: 3\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The model receives representations that carry information about the tokens and their positions.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4.3 Common approaches\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Sinusoidal positional encoding:** Uses mathematical functions to represent positions.\n",
        "- **Learned positional embeddings:** Learns position representations during training.\n",
        "- **RoPE (Rotary Positional Embeddings):** Incorporates positional information through transformations used in attention.\n",
        "\n",
        "Different Transformer architectures use different approaches.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:** Positional information helps the model account for token order.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 5: Transformer Layers\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the central part of many modern LLMs.\n",
        "\n",
        "The input representations pass through multiple Transformer layers.\n",
        "\n",
        "Each layer transforms the representations to help the model capture relationships and patterns in the input.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simplified Transformer layer looks like this:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "        Input representations\n",
        "                  |\n",
        "                  ▼\n",
        "       ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "       │  A. Self-Attention │\n",
        "       └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "       ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "       │ B. Residual        │\n",
        "       │    Connection      │\n",
        "       │    + Normalization │\n",
        "       └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "       ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "       │ C. Feed-Forward    │\n",
        "       │    Network (FFN)   │\n",
        "       └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "       ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "       │ D. Residual        │\n",
        "       │    Connection      │\n",
        "       │    + Normalization │\n",
        "       └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "         Output representations\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "          Next Transformer layer\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Now let's understand each component separately.\n",
        "\n",
        "## STEP 5A: Self-Attention\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is self-attention?\n",
        "\n",
        "Self-attention allows a token's representation to incorporate information from other relevant tokens in its context.\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "The cat sat on the mat.\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "When processing the token `sat`, information from `cat` may help the model understand who performed the action.\n",
        "\n",
        "Attention helps the model learn such relationships.\n",
        "\n",
        "### How does attention work?\n",
        "\n",
        "Self-attention uses three vectors:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Query (Q)\n",
        "- Key (K)\n",
        "- Value (V)\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's understand them individually.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Query (Q)\n",
        "\n",
        "The Query represents what information a token is looking for when calculating attention.\n",
        "\n",
        "Think of it as asking:\n",
        "\n",
        "> \"What information is relevant to me?\"\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Key (K)\n",
        "\n",
        "The Key represents information used to determine how relevant a token is to another token's Query.\n",
        "\n",
        "Think of it as asking:\n",
        "\n",
        "> \"How relevant might my information be to that request?\"\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Value (V)\n",
        "\n",
        "The Value carries the information that gets combined according to the attention weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "Think of it as:\n",
        "\n",
        "> \"What information can I contribute?\"\n",
        "\n",
        "These are conceptual explanations; mathematically, Q, K, and V are vectors calculated using learned linear transformations.\n",
        "\n",
        "### How are Q, K, and V used?\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Input representations\n",
        "          |\n",
        "          ▼\n",
        "    Linear projections\n",
        "          |\n",
        "     ┌────┼────┐\n",
        "     ▼    ▼    ▼\n",
        "     Q    K    V\n",
        "     |    |    |\n",
        "     └────┤    |\n",
        "          ▼    |\n",
        "   Attention scores\n",
        "          |\n",
        "          ▼\n",
        "   Softmax → Attention weights\n",
        "          |\n",
        "          ▼\n",
        "  Weighted combination of V\n",
        "          |\n",
        "          ▼\n",
        "   Attention output\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The attention score determines how much attention one token gives to another, subject to the architecture's attention rules.\n",
        "\n",
        "A common scaled dot-product attention formula is:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "\\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}\n",
        "\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "Where:\n",
        "- \\(Q\\) represents Queries.\n",
        "- \\(K\\) represents Keys.\n",
        "- \\(V\\) represents Values.\n",
        "- \\(d_k\\) is the dimension of each Key vector.\n",
        "\n",
        "You don't need to memorize the formula yet. First understand the flow.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What does attention produce?\n",
        "\n",
        "It produces updated representations containing information gathered from relevant tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:** In a typical decoder-only LLM, a causal attention mask prevents a token from attending to future tokens during next-token training and generation.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## STEP 5B: Residual Connections and Layer Normalization\n",
        "\n",
        "After attention, the Transformer applies additional operations to help train deep neural networks.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Residual connection\n",
        "\n",
        "A residual connection adds an earlier representation to a transformed representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Simplified:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Original input ───────────────┐\n",
        "      |                       |\n",
        "      ▼                       |\n",
        "Attention / transformation    |\n",
        "      |                       |\n",
        "      └───────────┬───────────┘\n",
        "                  ▼\n",
        "             Add together\n",
        "                  |\n",
        "                  ▼\n",
        "             Output\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Conceptually:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "\\text{Output}=\\text{Input}+\\text{Transformation}(\\text{Input})\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "This helps information and gradients flow through the network.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Layer normalization\n",
        "\n",
        "Layer normalization normalizes activations according to a learned normalization scheme.\n",
        "\n",
        "It helps keep the network's computations well-behaved during training.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why use them?\n",
        "\n",
        "They help make deep Transformer networks easier to train.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:** The exact order of attention, residual connections, and normalization varies by architecture. The diagram shows a simplified arrangement.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## STEP 5C: Feed-Forward Network (FFN)\n",
        "\n",
        "After attention, the representation passes through a feed-forward network.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What does it do?\n",
        "\n",
        "Attention gathers information from other tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "The feed-forward network then transforms each token's representation using learned parameters and nonlinear functions.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simplified structure is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Input representation\n",
        "         |\n",
        "         ▼\n",
        "    Linear layer\n",
        "         |\n",
        "         ▼\n",
        " Activation function\n",
        "    (e.g., GELU)\n",
        "         |\n",
        "         ▼\n",
        "    Linear layer\n",
        "         |\n",
        "         ▼\n",
        " Transformed representation\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "A simplified mathematical expression is:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "\\operatorname{FFN}(x)=W_2 f(W_1x+b_1)+b_2\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "Here:\n",
        "- \\(x\\) is the input representation.\n",
        "- \\(W_1\\) and \\(W_2\\) are learned weight matrices.\n",
        "- \\(b_1\\) and \\(b_2\\) are biases.\n",
        "- \\(f\\) is an activation function.\n",
        "\n",
        "Modern architectures may use different activation functions or gated feed-forward designs.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why is the FFN important?\n",
        "\n",
        "It helps the model transform information into increasingly useful representations.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Attention combines information across tokens.\n",
        "- The FFN transforms each token's representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## STEP 5D: Residual Connection and Normalization After the FFN\n",
        "\n",
        "After the feed-forward network, another residual connection and normalization operation are typically applied.\n",
        "\n",
        "Simplified:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Input to FFN\n",
        "     |\n",
        "     ▼\n",
        "Feed-Forward Network\n",
        "     |\n",
        "     ▼\n",
        "Transformed representation\n",
        "     |\n",
        "     +──────── Original FFN input\n",
        "     |                   |\n",
        "     └────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "              ▼\n",
        "         Add together\n",
        "              |\n",
        "              ▼\n",
        "       Normalization\n",
        "              |\n",
        "              ▼\n",
        "       Layer output\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This output is passed to the next Transformer layer.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What happens when there are many layers?\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Embedding + Positional Information\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "         Transformer Layer 1\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "         Transformer Layer 2\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "         Transformer Layer 3\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "                ...\n",
        "                 |\n",
        "                 ▼\n",
        "         Transformer Layer N\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Each layer transforms the representations further.\n",
        "\n",
        "A model may have dozens or more layers, depending on its architecture.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:** Steps 5A, 5B, 5C, and 5D are components of a Transformer layer. They are not four separate training stages.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 6: Linear Output Layer\n",
        "\n",
        "After the final Transformer layer, the model needs to produce scores for possible next tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "The linear output layer does this.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the context is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "The cat sat on the\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The model's final representation is passed through an output projection.\n",
        "\n",
        "Conceptually:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Final Transformer representations\n",
        "               |\n",
        "               ▼\n",
        "        Linear output layer\n",
        "               |\n",
        "               ▼\n",
        "             Logits\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "### What are logits?\n",
        "\n",
        "Logits are raw numerical scores assigned to possible next tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine the model's vocabulary contains these candidate tokens:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Candidate token | Illustrative logit |\n",
        "|---|---:|\n",
        "| mat | 4.2 |\n",
        "| floor | 2.8 |\n",
        "| dog | 1.1 |\n",
        "| banana | -0.5 |\n",
        "\n",
        "These numbers are illustrative, not actual model outputs.\n",
        "\n",
        "A higher logit generally means the token receives a higher probability after applying softmax, all else being equal.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why do we need this layer?\n",
        "\n",
        "The Transformer creates contextual representations. The output layer maps those representations to scores over the model's vocabulary.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a vocabulary containing 50,000 tokens, the output layer typically produces approximately 50,000 scores for the next-token prediction.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:**\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Final Transformer representation → Linear layer → Logits\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 7: Softmax\n",
        "\n",
        "We now have logits, but logits are not probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "Softmax converts them into a probability distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the logits are:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Token | Logit |\n",
        "|---|---:|\n",
        "| mat | 4.2 |\n",
        "| floor | 2.8 |\n",
        "| dog | 1.1 |\n",
        "| banana | -0.5 |\n",
        "\n",
        "After applying softmax, imagine we get:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Token | Illustrative probability |\n",
        "|---|---:|\n",
        "| mat | 0.72 |\n",
        "| floor | 0.20 |\n",
        "| dog | 0.06 |\n",
        "| banana | 0.02 |\n",
        "| **Total** | **1.00** |\n",
        "\n",
        "These probabilities are illustrative.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What does this mean?\n",
        "\n",
        "Based on the current context and model, `mat` is the most probable candidate among these examples.\n",
        "\n",
        "Softmax is calculated using:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "P_i=\\frac{e^{z_i}}{\\sum_j e^{z_j}}\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "Where:\n",
        "- \\(z_i\\) is the logit for a particular token.\n",
        "- \\(e^{z_i}\\) converts the score into a positive value.\n",
        "- The denominator normalizes the values so the probabilities sum to 1.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:**\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Logits → Softmax → Probabilities\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 8: Next-Token Prediction\n",
        "\n",
        "The model now has probabilities for possible next tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "During training, the model's prediction is compared with the actual next token in the training example.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Training sentence:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "The cat sat on the mat.\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the context is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "The cat sat on the\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The correct next token is:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "mat\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The model might predict:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "mat    → 0.72\n",
        "floor  → 0.20\n",
        "dog    → 0.06\n",
        "banana → 0.02\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The correct next token is `mat`, and the model assigns it a probability of `0.72`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The model is not yet perfectly certain, so the training process can use the prediction error to improve its parameters.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What happens next?\n",
        "\n",
        "The prediction is passed to the loss calculation.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important distinction:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Training:** The correct next token is known from the training data, so the prediction can be evaluated.\n",
        "- **Inference:** The model does not normally have the correct next token supplied in advance. It uses its output probabilities to select or sample a token.\n",
        "\n",
        "Also, the model does not need to choose its final token during training in the same way it does during generation. Training commonly calculates probabilities and loss directly from the logits.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 9: Loss Calculation\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is loss?\n",
        "\n",
        "Loss is a numerical measure of how poorly the model predicts the correct target.\n",
        "\n",
        "For next-token training, a common loss function is **cross-entropy loss**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example A: Better prediction\n",
        "\n",
        "Correct next token:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "mat\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Model assigns:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "P(mat) = 0.90\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The loss for this prediction is:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "L=-\\log(0.90)\\approx 0.105\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "The loss is relatively low.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example B: Worse prediction\n",
        "\n",
        "Correct next token:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "mat\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Model assigns:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "P(mat) = 0.10\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The loss is:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "L=-\\log(0.10)\\approx 2.303\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "The loss is much higher.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What do we learn from this?\n",
        "\n",
        "| Probability assigned to the correct token | Loss |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| High probability | Low loss |\n",
        "| Low probability | High loss |\n",
        "\n",
        "The model is trained to reduce its loss over many examples.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is the actual training objective?\n",
        "\n",
        "For a sequence, the model commonly predicts each next token and calculates a loss for each prediction. These losses are aggregated, often using an average over the relevant tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Input context       Correct next token\n",
        "--------------------------------------\n",
        "The                 cat\n",
        "The cat             sat\n",
        "The cat sat         on\n",
        "The cat sat on      the\n",
        "The cat sat on the  mat\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The model is trained to assign higher probabilities to the correct next tokens across the sequence.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:** Loss tells us how well the predictions match the training targets. It does not directly tell us whether an entire answer is factually correct.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 10: Backpropagation\n",
        "\n",
        "Now we know the loss, but how does the model learn from it?\n",
        "\n",
        "The answer is **backpropagation**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What does backpropagation do?\n",
        "\n",
        "Backpropagation calculates gradients that indicate how changes in model parameters would affect the loss.\n",
        "\n",
        "The parameters include learned weights in components such as:\n",
        "- Embedding layers\n",
        "- Attention projections\n",
        "- Feed-forward networks\n",
        "- Output layers\n",
        "\n",
        "### The process\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Forward pass\n",
        "     |\n",
        "     ▼\n",
        "Model predicts tokens\n",
        "     |\n",
        "     ▼\n",
        "Loss is calculated\n",
        "     |\n",
        "     ▼\n",
        "Backpropagation\n",
        "     |\n",
        "     ▼\n",
        "Gradients are calculated\n",
        "     |\n",
        "     ▼\n",
        "Optimizer uses the gradients\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is a gradient?\n",
        "\n",
        "A gradient indicates how the loss changes with respect to a parameter.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "This represents the rate at which the loss changes as the parameter \\(w\\) changes.\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine one parameter has:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Parameter: w = 0.50\n",
        "\n",
        "Gradient:  +0.80\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The positive gradient indicates that increasing this parameter locally would increase the loss, assuming other conditions stay fixed.\n",
        "\n",
        "The optimizer uses this information to decide how to update the parameter.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Does backpropagation itself update the weights?\n",
        "\n",
        "**No.**\n",
        "\n",
        "Backpropagation calculates gradients.\n",
        "\n",
        "The optimizer uses those gradients to update the weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "That distinction is important.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 11: Optimizer Update\n",
        "\n",
        "The model has calculated gradients.\n",
        "\n",
        "Now the optimizer uses them to update the model's parameters.\n",
        "\n",
        "An optimizer commonly used in LLM training is **Adam or one of its variants**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Simplified example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a parameter has:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Current weight = 0.50\n",
        "Gradient       = +0.80\n",
        "Learning rate  = 0.01\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "With a simple gradient descent update:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "w_{\\text{new}}=w_{\\text{old}}-\\eta g\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "Where:\n",
        "- \\(w_{\\text{old}}\\) is the current weight.\n",
        "- \\(\\eta\\) is the learning rate.\n",
        "- \\(g\\) is the gradient.\n",
        "\n",
        "Substituting the values:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "w_{\\text{new}}=0.50-(0.01\\times0.80)\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "w_{\\text{new}}=0.492\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "The parameter decreases from `0.50` to `0.492`.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a simplified example using gradient descent, not a complete Adam update.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What changes during training?\n",
        "\n",
        "The optimizer updates model parameters, potentially including:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Embedding weights\n",
        "- Query, Key, and Value projection weights\n",
        "- Feed-forward network weights\n",
        "- Output projection weights\n",
        "- Other learned parameters\n",
        "\n",
        "The exact parameters updated depend on the training configuration.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is the learning rate?\n",
        "\n",
        "The learning rate controls the scale of parameter updates.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Too large: training may become unstable.\n",
        "- Too small: training may progress very slowly.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Remember:**\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Backpropagation → Gradients\n",
        "Optimizer + Gradients → Updated weights\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 12: Repeat Training\n",
        "\n",
        "One example is not enough to train a useful LLM.\n",
        "\n",
        "The model processes many examples across many training steps.\n",
        "\n",
        "The core process repeats:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "          ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "          │   Training batch   │\n",
        "          └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                    ▼\n",
        "          ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "          │    Forward pass    │\n",
        "          │ Generate logits    │\n",
        "          └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                    ▼\n",
        "          ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "          │   Loss calculation │\n",
        "          └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                    ▼\n",
        "          ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "          │  Backpropagation   │\n",
        "          │ Calculate gradients│\n",
        "          └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                    ▼\n",
        "          ┌────────────────────┐\n",
        "          │  Optimizer update  │\n",
        "          │ Update parameters  │\n",
        "          └─────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                    │\n",
        "                    ▼\n",
        "          Process another batch\n",
        "                    │\n",
        "                    └─────────────┐\n",
        "                                  │\n",
        "                                  ▼\n",
        "                         Repeat training\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is a batch?\n",
        "\n",
        "A batch is a group of training examples or sequences processed together.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is a training step?\n",
        "\n",
        "A training step typically includes:\n",
        "1. A forward pass.\n",
        "2. Loss calculation.\n",
        "3. Backpropagation.\n",
        "4. An optimizer update.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What is an epoch?\n",
        "\n",
        "An epoch is one complete pass through the training dataset, although the exact meaning depends on how the training data is organized and sampled.\n",
        "\n",
        "Large-scale LLM training can involve enormous datasets and many training steps.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What happens over time?\n",
        "\n",
        "The training process adjusts the parameters to improve the model's predictions on the training objective.\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal is to learn useful patterns that generalize beyond the examples seen during training—not simply to memorize every sentence.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# STEP 13: What Happens After Pretraining?\n",
        "\n",
        "Pretraining creates a base model, but that does not automatically make it a polished assistant.\n",
        "\n",
        "Many LLMs undergo additional stages.\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "        ┌─────────────────────┐\n",
        "        │    PRETRAINING      │\n",
        "        │ Learn general       │\n",
        "        │ language patterns   │\n",
        "        └──────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                   ▼\n",
        "        ┌─────────────────────┐\n",
        "        │ SUPERVISED          │\n",
        "        │ FINE-TUNING (SFT)   │\n",
        "        │ Learn from example  │\n",
        "        │ instructions/answers│\n",
        "        └──────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                   ▼\n",
        "        ┌─────────────────────┐\n",
        "        │ PREFERENCE TRAINING │\n",
        "        │ Learn preferred     │\n",
        "        │ response behavior   │\n",
        "        └──────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                   ▼\n",
        "        ┌─────────────────────┐\n",
        "        │ OPTIONAL ALIGNMENT  │\n",
        "        │ RLHF, DPO, or other │\n",
        "        │ methods             │\n",
        "        └──────────┬──────────┘\n",
        "                   ▼\n",
        "        ┌─────────────────────┐\n",
        "        │ DEPLOYED LLM        │\n",
        "        │ Ready for inference │\n",
        "        └─────────────────────┘\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "These stages are not identical across all models. Some models use different combinations of supervised fine-tuning, preference optimization, and reinforcement learning.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Final Summary: Everything in the Correct Order\n",
        "\n",
        "Here is the complete sequence to remember:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "TRAINING DATA\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "TOKENIZATION\n",
        "Text → Tokens → Token IDs\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "EMBEDDING LOOKUP\n",
        "Token IDs → Learned vectors\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "POSITIONAL INFORMATION\n",
        "Represent token order\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "TRANSFORMER LAYERS\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ├── A. Self-Attention\n",
        "      |      ├── Query (Q)\n",
        "      |      ├── Key (K)\n",
        "      |      ├── Value (V)\n",
        "      |      ├── Attention scores\n",
        "      |      └── Attention weights\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ├── B. Residual Connection\n",
        "      |      + Layer Normalization\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ├── C. Feed-Forward Network\n",
        "      |      + Activation Function\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      └── D. Residual Connection\n",
        "             + Layer Normalization\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "LINEAR OUTPUT LAYER\n",
        "Representations → Logits\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "SOFTMAX\n",
        "Logits → Probabilities\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "NEXT-TOKEN PREDICTION\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "LOSS CALCULATION\n",
        "Compare predictions with correct targets\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "BACKPROPAGATION\n",
        "Calculate gradients\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "OPTIMIZER UPDATE\n",
        "Adjust model weights\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "REPEAT TRAINING\n",
        "Process more batches and training steps\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "TRAINED BASE MODEL\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "OPTIONAL POST-TRAINING\n",
        "SFT / Preference Optimization / RLHF / DPO\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "DEPLOYED MODEL\n",
        "      |\n",
        "      ▼\n",
        "INFERENCE\n",
        "Use learned weights to generate responses\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## Test your understanding\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's check your understanding before moving to the next topic.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question 1:** The model predicts the correct next token, `mat`, with a probability of `0.10`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Which component calculates how wrong that prediction is?\n",
        "\n",
        "**A.** Tokenization  \n",
        "**B.** Loss calculation  \n",
        "**C.** Embedding lookup  \n",
        "**D.** Positional information\n",
        "\n",
        "Choose one answer, and we will continue step by step."
